# Chromium + Playwright + PDF tooling, for cerebro.
#
# WHY CEREBRO NEEDS ONE AT ALL. Two reasons, and only one of them is Google.
#
#  1. The OAuth consent for each Google account has to happen where a browser is, and
#     cerebro is where the tokens live. A HEADLESS browser does not solve this: Google
#     requires a password and, usually, a second factor — a human has to drive it. So the
#     image carries a small X desktop and noVNC as well, which means the owner opens a URL
#     from his own machine and clicks through the consent against THIS host. The
#     alternative (run the flow on Workhorse, copy the token across) works too, but it
#     puts a mailbox credential in two places and adds a copy step that can rot.
#
#  2. Playwright is the missing capability for Jarvis generally: pages that render with
#     JavaScript are invisible to the http-search path, and Chromium can print a page to
#     PDF, which is what a document pipeline needs when it is handed a URL.
#
# WHY NOT FLATPAK, which is this fleet's route for applications. Cerebro has no flatpak
# and no distrobox — it is rpm-ostree with podman, and layering a browser would reboot the
# box that runs the brain, mail-api and the panel. See [[fleet-packaging-policy]].
#
# PDF TOOLS ARE HERE TOO, AND THE HOST HAS NONE. `pdftotext`, `qpdf` and `gs` are all
# absent from cerebro, which is what the separate jarvis-ocr image was built to work
# around. This image carries its own copy so a URL-to-PDF or a PDF-splitting job does not
# need a third container.
#
# Build (context is the repo root, same as the OCR image):
#
#     podman build -t localhost/jarvis-browser -f sandbox/Containerfile.browser .
#
# Run it interactively with sandbox/run-browser.sh, which starts Xvfb, a window manager,
# x11vnc and noVNC and prints the URL to open. Playwright works headlessly in the same
# image with no display at all.
FROM docker.io/library/python:3.12-slim

ENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
RUN apt-get update \
 && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        # the PDF toolbox the host does not have
        poppler-utils \
        qpdf \
        ghostscript \
        # fonts, or every rendered page is a wall of tofu
        fonts-liberation \
        fonts-dejavu-core \
        # the small desktop that makes this a browser somebody can actually use
        xvfb \
        x11vnc \
        novnc \
        websockify \
        fluxbox \
        xterm \
 && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# Playwright installs its own Chromium build — the one it has been tested against — and
# --with-deps pulls the shared libraries that build needs. Installing Debian's chromium
# instead would give a browser Playwright cannot drive without version pinning, which is
# the whole reason to use Playwright.
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir playwright \
 && playwright install --with-deps chromium \
 && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

COPY sandbox/browser-entrypoint.sh /usr/local/bin/browser-entrypoint.sh
RUN chmod +x /usr/local/bin/browser-entrypoint.sh

ENV DISPLAY=:99 \
    SCREEN=1600x1000x24 \
    NOVNC_PORT=6080
EXPOSE 6080

ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/browser-entrypoint.sh"]
